592186125517 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "21ce271d-74a1-526d-a3bb-1e1dc0da83ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125517 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125518 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125518 #edb/ref 1061 30778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125518 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6b214232-840a-58b5-8946-bbbc69c878d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125518 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125519 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125519 #edb/ref 1061 30779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125519 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e4c9032-faa3-5e93-a91c-a0a7db82838a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125519 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125520 #edb/ref 1061 30781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125520 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "595c41f8-1c2f-5e89-b663-e36a44f691ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125520 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125526 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125526 #edb/ref 1061 30810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125526 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3f461a0c-aa8c-58dd-b9c4-e57ae5d51a3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125526 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125644 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125644 #edb/ref 1061 45321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125644 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2800e74a-ab30-5499-809e-31fb6deb9785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125644 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125646 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125646 #edb/ref 1061 45325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125646 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8fa4a35a-4cb0-5fd5-99f4-903b12b2986b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125646 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125647 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125647 #edb/ref 1061 45326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125647 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "85399770-a72b-521d-b90e-0e8d27d7e807" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125647 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125830 #edb/ref 1064 "A **cylinder** is a surface made by the set of lines that pass through a plane curve $C$ in the $xy$-plane and are perpendicular to this plane. The curve $C$ is called the **base curve**.\n\nThe familiar two-dimensional conic sections (circles, ellipses, parabolas, and hyperbolas) can be used to describe cylinders. In this lesson, we'll explore these shapes.\n\nAn **elliptic cylinder** is a cylinder whose base curve $C$ is an ellipse. If the ellipse lies in the $xy$-plane and is centered at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of our elliptic cylinder is given by\n\n$$\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} = 1,$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the semi-axes of the ellipse $C.$ In particular, if $a=b$, we get a **circular cylinder.**\n\n**Watch out!** This may look like the equation of a regular ellipse. However, since we're now describing a surface in three-dimensional space, we assume that the $z$-coordinate is arbitrary. Thus, if we want $z$ to feature in our equations explicitly, we would write\n\n$$\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} = 1, \\qquad z=k$$ where $k\\in \\mathbb R.$\n\nFor example, the graph of the elliptic cylinder\n\n$$\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + y^2 = 1$$\n\nis shown below. The center of its base ellipse $C$ is at the origin, and the **axis** of the elliptic cylinder (the line through the center of the cylinder) is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/50/504e609e134654d258378226d90f5faa8b9f6d12313a4fcac4a23d0bfe6bea52.png)\n\nLet's describe some properties of this elliptic cylinder:\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation $$\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + y^2 = 1,$$ which is the base ellipse $C$ centered at the origin $(0,0,0)$ with semi-major and semi-minor axes of lengths $2$ and $1,$ respectively.\n\n![](images/d0/d0e4c0630b0da60c4324330211ab1bf8090ebec067d9ebc2ac2a03056c500bd6.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to two lines in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/38/3813a5f43319056be03ddd8b3353ef3a8166b7495b32397c91066f40d0b33cd5.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to two lines in the $yz$-plane.\n\n![](images/a0/a0d928060ad18d3ba677f5dd184777b833d3abf03226c04c21292e455bcd0518.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125830 #edb/ref 1065 4931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125830 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f8f004e2-d43f-5628-bbbe-4b3c7918a9f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125830 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125831 #edb/ref 1064 "A **parabolic cylinder** is a cylinder whose base curve $C$ is a parabola.\n\nIf the parabola $C$ opens upward or downward when viewed from the $xy$-plane and its vertex is at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the parabolic cylinder is given by $$(x-x_0)^2 = 4p(y-y_0),$$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\nFor example, the graph of the parabolic cylinder $$x^2 = y$$ is shown below. Its base curve is an upward-opening parabola in the $xy$-plane with its vertex at the origin. The **axis** of the cylinder is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/03/03ab0a28802900d8076fdd5f555bdad6582c449441c46f1de74260013cc55119.png)\n\nThe trace of this parabolic cylinder can be either a parabola or a set of lines.\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation $$x^2 = y,$$ which corresponds to the base parabola $C$ with its vertex at the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/98/98124890d921c73d0cda54428ac803221d5f393384e14db2909b0138fda0fb93.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to a line in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/f8/f87dbd180911db28a515dfe11da99d5d9d06da74e7ef117ea0a6df88018e95a2.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2} & =y \\\\ y & =0,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to a line in the $yz$-plane.\n\n![](images/f8/f87dbd180911db28a515dfe11da99d5d9d06da74e7ef117ea0a6df88018e95a2.png)\n\nOn the other hand, if the base parabola $C$ is left-opening or right-opening when viewed from the $xy$-plane and its vertex is at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the parabolic cylinder is given by $$(y-y_0)^2 = 4p(x-x_0),$$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\nFor example, the graph of the parabolic cylinder $$y^2 = x$$ is shown below. Its base curve is a right-opening parabola in the $xy$-plane with its vertex at the origin. The cylinder's axis is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/a8/a87267dbd77b1561c047c6228ba0c70b08e5a48228d7e0b6a161463045d5e22b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125831 #edb/ref 1065 4932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125831 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4026d216-b14f-558d-a7a4-cf39aa550b45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125831 #edb/ref 1067 "Parabolic Cylinders" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125834 #edb/ref 1064 "A **hyperbolic cylinder** is a cylinder whose base curve $C$ is a hyperbola.\n\nIf the hyperbola $C$ is horizontal in the $xy$-plane and is centered at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the hyperbolic cylinder is given by $$\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} = 1,$$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperbola cylinder $$x^2 - y^2 = 1$$ is shown below. Its base curve is a horizontal hyperbola in the $xy$-plane centered at the origin. The **axis** of the cylinder is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/e3/e3c921cc7d6a1edbb182ecf158d7b84eb0d203fd20924d07cf57ca8f8ffdc107.png)\n\nThe trace of this surface can be either a hyperbola, a set of lines, or the empty set.\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation $$x^2 - y^2 = 1,$$ which is the base hyperbola $C$ centered at the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/19/19e02f27f625dd75d12333de3b82a7a012e4f6370c729eef5cd38a633c2c8689.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to a pair of lines in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/56/5679b53433493d8b6e92d5aa5273be30bbf67a9db51993a925423d8514571f05.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is the empty set.\n\nOn the other hand, if the base hyperbola $C$ is vertical when viewed from the $xy$-plane and is centered at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the hyperbolic cylinder is given by $$\\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} = 1,$$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperbola cylinder $$y^2 - x^2 = 1$$ is shown below. Its base curve is a vertical hyperbola in the $xy$-plane centered at the origin, and the cylinder's axis is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/d6/d690bca9515389bb403d0f205861af3294bc3a961df6691a5adb86de01421cff.png)\n\nNote that for all of the cylinders discussed so far, the *domain* of each cylinder in the $xy$-plane is simply the base curve $C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125834 #edb/ref 1065 4939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125834 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7260540c-2b7c-555f-9aa0-6f3dd490079b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125834 #edb/ref 1067 "Hyperbolic Cylinders" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125884 #edb/ref 1064 "So far, we've encountered cylinders whose axes are parallel to the $z$-axis. However, we can also define cylinders whose axes are parallel to the $x$- or $y$-axes. To do this, we simply need to describe a base curve in either the $yz$-plane or the $xz$-plane. \n\nFor an elliptic cylinder whose base ellipse $C$ is centered at the origin, the other two cases are as follows:\n\n- Consider the elliptic cylinder $C$ whose base curve lies in the $xz$-plane.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} = 1\n$$\n\nIn this case, the axis of the elliptic cylinder is the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f3/f32db327a6e1f54d5b346af4ea2f9b0bcfce7f82c1b6e44375f398705acd4f63.png)\n\n- Consider the elliptic cylinder $C$ whose base curve lies in the $yz$-plane.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} = 1\n$$\n\nIn this case, the axis of the elliptic cylinder is the $x$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/53/5395c07f7646db4d4091e4bf176ae8d13c9c6753c322b8325dee2542480efdd4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125884 #edb/ref 1065 7851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125884 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "12af86a6-3212-596e-a78e-92fb9ba06344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125884 #edb/ref 1067 "Elliptic Cylinders With Alternative Central Axes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125886 #edb/ref 1064 "For a parabolic cylinder whose base parabola $C$ has its vertex at the origin, the other four cases are as follows:\n\n- When $C$ is in the $xz$-plane and $p>0,$ the axis is the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/79/79d1fdd86e7f615597125f5ac43e55ecd59bc298387575707348784d2d48acd0.png)\n\nNote that if $p < 0,$ we get a reflection in the $xy$-plane or $yz$-plane of the parabolic cylinders shown above.\n\n- When $C$ is in the $yz$-plane and $p>0,$ the axis is the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/73/730dccdd02e06d5f2de834f7b42c9121246aacda7595d2c5f760ae2f4c3bb5c9.png)\n\nNote that if $p<0,$ we get a reflection in the $xy$-plane or $xz$-plane of the parabolic cylinders shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125886 #edb/ref 1065 7855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125886 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "049a63bf-b3e2-52e8-80c1-82042790f8f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125886 #edb/ref 1067 "Parabolic Cylinders With Alternative Central Axes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125887 #edb/ref 1064 "For a hyperbolic cylinder whose base hyperbola $C$ has its vertex at the origin, the other four cases are as follows:\n\n- When $C$ is in the $xz$-plane, the axis of the hyperbolic cylinder is the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/28/28f79c28aa6dfaa28b7b8ddd6d196bcb21db18411454c655a3365be52e8c6bc7.png)\n\n- When $C$ is in the $yz$-plane, the axis of the hyperbolic cylinder is the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/57/5763cb9b167c2e1e9d17c9778bf569a0efafb4fe36f97e05e8e2173fb29fc998.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125887 #edb/ref 1065 7856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125887 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dba66b44-ad67-5d83-b191-e1d2f8b59ff8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125887 #edb/ref 1067 "Hyperbolic Cylinders With Alternative Central Axes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129177 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4de868c0-80b7-5cbb-9dc2-ba04493471fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129178 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129178 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129178 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129178 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129178 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129178 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129178 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129178 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129178 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129178 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129178 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129178 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129178 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129178 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129178 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129178 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129180 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cc56f7a5-65a7-53a9-9eb6-ae7b91a1bdd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129328 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129328 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129328 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129328 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129328 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129328 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129328 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129328 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129328 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129328 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129328 #edb/ref 1045 "Tangent Planes to Surfaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129328 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129328 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129328 #edb/ref 1050 1260.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186129328 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b14c5a1b-dfd3-54ea-b205-2433540d7060" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130044 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130044 #edb/ref 1061 23115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130044 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "41594163-d4dc-5179-be5c-4adec6fac7bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130044 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130045 #edb/ref 1061 23116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130045 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0a78a51-4800-5622-8fa8-92069e9e72f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130045 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130046 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130046 #edb/ref 1061 23118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130046 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6b19e61c-814d-510b-93fb-964a9df12d5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130046 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130047 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130047 #edb/ref 1061 23123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130047 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a126e19a-f8b5-5040-9719-0bc0a4c611e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130047 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130049 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130049 #edb/ref 1061 23133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130049 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "828fdb16-4d33-565c-9b6f-4db12aed5567" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130049 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130050 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130050 #edb/ref 1061 23134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130050 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "773e70e4-737a-5fa4-92ed-0e1d04815ea8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130050 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130051 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130051 #edb/ref 1061 23135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130051 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ca4b822d-4434-5d75-8400-5106f71e78a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130051 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130052 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130052 #edb/ref 1061 23153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130052 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0355e3e0-f89b-5dca-97bc-21791fa4aed2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130052 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130067 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130067 #edb/ref 1061 25668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130067 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0296b5b5-c2ce-56c6-a8e4-ad0af71e0dde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130067 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130382 #edb/ref 1064 "Earlier, we claimed that function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$ Let's now justify this claim.\n\nRecall that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=x_0$ with derivative $f'(x_0)$ if and only if the following limit exists:\n\n$$\nf'(x_0) = \\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(x_0)} {x-x_0}\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this limit as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}})-{f}^{'}({x}_{0}) & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}-{f}^{'}({x}_{0})) & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})-(x-{x}_{0}){f}^{'}({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the above limit to hold true, the numerator must approach zero. So, near $x_0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) - (x-x_0)f'(x_0) \\approx 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) \\approx (x-x_0)f'(x_0).\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following equation of the tangent line at $x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny -y_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x}(x_0)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can say that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$\n\nWe'll now use a similar idea to define the differentiability of a multivariable function at a point $P$ in terms of tangent planes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130382 #edb/ref 1065 3914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130382 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "48f4e466-1052-5e98-b9a0-d345e3139cb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130382 #edb/ref 1067 "Defining the Differentiability of Single Variable Functions in Terms of Tangent Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130383 #edb/ref 1064 "Earlier, we claimed that function $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at $(x_0,y_0)$ *if the function admits a unique tangent plane* at $(x_0,y_0).$ Let's now justify this claim.\n\nAssume that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function of two variables. Since $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at $(x_0,y_0),$ then there exists a vector $\\nabla f = \\langle f_1, f_2\\rangle$ such that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (x_0,y_0)}\\dfrac {f(x,y) - f(x_0,y_0) - \\langle x-x_0, y-y_0\\rangle \\cdot \\langle f_1, f_2\\rangle} {||\\langle x-x_0,y-y_0\\rangle||} = 0. \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFor the above limit to hold, the numerator must approach zero. So, near $(x_0,y_0),$ we must have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) - f(x_0,y_0) - \\langle x-x_0, y-y_0\\rangle \\cdot \\langle f_1, f_2\\rangle \\approx 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x,y) - f(x_0,y_0) \\approx \\langle x-x_0, y-y_0\\rangle \\cdot \\langle f_1, f_2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nExpanding the dot product, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx  f(x_0,y_0) + (x-x_0)\\cdot f_1+ (y-y_0)\\cdot f_2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to show that $f_1$ and $f_2$ are the partial derivatives.\n\n- Let $y=y_0$ in the first equation $(\\ast)$ above. This gives\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\dfrac {f(x,y_0) - f(x_0,y_0) - (x-x_0) f_1} {x-x_0} = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is the definition of the partial derivative with respect to $x.$ So, we have $f_1 = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0).$\n\n- Similarly, letting $x = x_0$ gives $f_2 = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x,y)$ can be linearized as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)\\approx f(x_0,y_0) + (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nBy setting $z=f(x,y)$ and $z_0 = f(x_0,y_0),$ we get the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can say that a function $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at $(x_0,y_0)$ *if the function admits a unique tangent plane* at $(x_0, y_0).$\n\nFinally, note that we have also shown that \n\n$$\n\\nabla f = \\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}, \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\nabla f$ is known as the **gradient vector**." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130383 #edb/ref 1065 3915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130383 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3e6cb319-460f-5a22-888e-e3f886ea9b0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130383 #edb/ref 1067 "Defining the Differentiability of Multivariable Functions in Terms of Tangent Planes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130384 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z = f(x,y)$ at $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be given as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nSlightly rewriting the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0)} \\cdot (x-x_0) + {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)} \\cdot (y-y_0) + ({\\color{blue}-1}) \\cdot (z - z_0) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich immediately gives us a normal vector to the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0)}, \\:\\: {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)}, \\:\\: {\\color{blue}-1} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nA normal vector to the tangent plane to the surface is called a **normal vector to the surface**." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130384 #edb/ref 1065 3919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130384 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c5ebd9d6-c219-583a-8bca-87268b8aa8bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130384 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding a Normal Vector to a Surface" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130385 #edb/ref 1064 "Until now, we considered only functions of the form \n\n![](images/48/4838bea41cd622ef69be5caaafac89df818d35cf108730686d68d7b7a12265ac.svg)\n\nof two independent variables. But what about the case of functions \n\n![](images/72/72e701a6fcd4514adb9731764fc74a1cb3f0b73f548680db162cd2c8a025e0d6.svg)\n\nwith three variables? Can we define a tangent plane to such a function?\n\nThe answer is yes! Analogously to the case with two variables, a function of three variables ![](images/72/72e701a6fcd4514adb9731764fc74a1cb3f0b73f548680db162cd2c8a025e0d6.svg) is differentiable at ![](images/f4/f46650c560f32308db30c404d86da2d5921654805cca770cd2bc3627e55dbf86.svg) if it admits a **unique tangent hyperplane** at ![](images/3f/3fe19998bf33d149c699a01ca363c2691041b3ecfd52c47a167053b4097dbb57.svg) where ![](images/7e/7e2db5c48c69709e3c1d04d4834af7cc0b2ff0df186cf1713e207c164a8e41e5.svg) The equation of the **tangent hyperplane** is given by\n\n![](images/f4/f433839875810dc7417400a22bad7b8a63c0eb1033954a8e91e7049f3ada361a.svg)\n\nNotice that we use the term *hyperplane*. The graphs of functions of three variables live in four-dimensional space. The equation above describes an object (a so-called hyperplane) in four-dimensional space that can be considered an analog to a *plane* in three-dimensional space." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130385 #edb/ref 1065 3920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130385 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d6f087f4-e5d4-59f8-90f7-a5a9484a0c6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130385 #edb/ref 1067 "The Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130386 #edb/ref 1064 "Similar to tangent lines for single variable functions, we can think of the **tangent plane** to a surface at a point as the plane that \"touches\" the surface at that point.\n\nThe equation of the tangent plane to a surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0),$ where $z_0=f(x_0,y_0),$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the surface \n\n$$\nz = 4-x^2-y^2.\n$$\n\nLet's find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface at the point $(0,\\sqrt{2},2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/03/03df90a94615238e2126338f64d1f51fcefc379068a8cdc366b33b998cd69f45.png)\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y) = (0,\\sqrt{2})\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,\\sqrt{2}) & =-2(0)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,\\sqrt{2}) & =-2(\\sqrt{2})=-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-2 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,\\sqrt{2})+(y-\\sqrt{2})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =0(x-0)-2\\sqrt{2}(y-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =-2\\sqrt{2}y+4 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}y+z-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane is $2\\sqrt{2}y + z - 6 = 0.$\n\n![](images/d5/d51bdbe8820472334f7e1d942c027b6ad8164eaece58220f3f2e5cdb51544f05.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130386 #edb/ref 1065 3921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130386 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bced0695-3ebd-5b62-b7ff-9c8f2500fc2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130386 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130396 #edb/ref 1064 "A surface $z = f(x,y)$ is differentiable at a point $P$ if and only if it admits a *unique* tangent plane at $P.$ Intuitively, this means that, in the neighborhood of $P,$ the surface $z=f(x,y)$ can be approximated as a plane.\n\nNote that this is analogous to the case of functions of one variable. A function $y = f(x)$ is differentiable at a point $P$ if and only if it admits a *unique* tangent *line* at $P.$ This is the same as saying that, in the neighborhood of $P,$ the curve $y=f(x)$ can be approximated as a straight line.  \n\nWe'll explore these ideas in more depth at the end of the lesson. For now, let's get some practice at computing tangent planes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130396 #edb/ref 1065 4231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130396 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "015c4e8e-c1ba-5672-8228-ff0869681d6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130396 #edb/ref 1067 "A Note on Differentiability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152198 #edb/ref 1038 #